  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Easter Pox Lichen (Pyrenula pseudobufonia) is a small, crustose lichen belonging to the Pyrenulaceae family, characterized by its dark appearance and small fruiting bodies. This species is found in New England and other regions of eastern North America, typically growing on bark substrates.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Perithecia appearance",
      "description": "Small, black, flask-shaped fruiting bodies (perithecia) embedded in the thallus with a distinctive ostiole (opening) at the apex",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Thallus color",
      "description": "Dark gray to nearly black crustose thallus that appears finely granular or warty in texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and distribution",
      "description": "Forms small colonies typically less than 2 cm in diameter, often scattered individually on bark",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Easter Pox Lichen grows on the bark of deciduous and coniferous trees in moist, shaded woodlands throughout New England.",
